course: dictionary
lesson: 7081
title: Pavement: the path or the road?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pavement is the path at the side of a road where people walk. «Walk carefully on the pavement» tells you it is for people, not for cars. The clues in a sentence help you work out the meaning, so look at what happens on the pavement.
explain: «A tiny coin on an empty pavement is different» shows you can walk along a pavement and find a coin on it. That fits the path meaning. In American English, pavement can mean the hard surface of a road, so always check which country the writer is from.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The pavement below was wet» makes sense if pavement means the path at the side of the road. Words can change from place to place, so a dictionary helps when you are not sure.
fact: A pavement is the path at the side of a road for people to walk on, in British English.
fact: In American English, pavement can mean the hard surface of a road, and the path is called a sidewalk.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, people often say footpath for the path at the side of the road.
fact: Tree roots can crack pavements, and wet autumn leaves on them can be as slippery as ice.
fact: Before you cross a road, stop at the kerb, then look and listen.
exercise: «Walk carefully on the pavement, because wet autumn leaves are as slippery as ice.» Where should people walk? | *On the pavement, at the side of the road | In the middle of the park | On the roof | On the road, with the cars
exercise: «Dandelion seeds float into pavement cracks, where dust and water collect.» What is a pavement here? | *A hard path at the side of a road | A tall tree | A soft hill of grass | A deep pond
exercise: Sam, from New York, says pavement means the hard surface of a road. What does pavement mean in American English? | A sandy beach | *The hard surface of a road | The path at the side of a road for walkers | A kind of bird
test: Why should you ride a scooter carefully on the pavement? | Pavements are only for cars | *Other people walk there, so you must watch out for them | Pavements are too hard to ride on | Scooters can fly over pavements
test: Before you cross a road, where should you stop? | In the middle of the road | *At the kerb, to look and listen | On the pavement's cracks
exam: Ella says «pavement» and Sam says «sidewalk» for the same kind of path. Which word means the path at the side of a road in British English? | Dandelion | Tarmac | *Pavement | Taproot
